            <ArticleTitle>Padua, the cradle of modern medicine: Bernardino Ramazzini (1633-1714) on headaches.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>169-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A prominent historian of Medicine, Henry Sigerist, quoted the Padua Medical School as &quot;the cradle of modern medicine&quot;. This opinion is currently accepted worldwide. A short outline on the contribution of the Padua Medical School to the development of medical knowledge in its &quot;golden age&quot; is given. In this context, the work of a prominent figure of the 17(th) century Padua University and the founder of Occupational Medicine, Bernardino Ramazzini, is considered, with focus on his interest in headache.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
